ACC-016 — Engineering Confidence

Document ID: ACC-016
Title: Engineering Confidence
Version: 1.0.0-draft
Status: Draft

1. Purpose

This specification defines Engineering Confidence, the quantified or qualified expression of certainty associated with engineering cognition within the Atlas Engineering Cognition Framework (AECF).

Engineering Confidence communicates the degree to which Engineering Evidence, Engineering Understanding, Engineering Reasoning, Engineering Capability outputs, and Decision Support can be relied upon, based on the available information and its evaluation.

2. Scope

This specification applies to all cognitive artifacts and processes within Atlas.

Engineering Confidence may be associated with:

Engineering Observations
Engineering Evidence
Engineering Understanding
Engineering Memory
Engineering Knowledge
Engineering Reasoning
Engineering Capability outputs
Decision Support
Engineering recommendations
Engineering predictions

Engineering Confidence applies throughout the engineering cognition lifecycle.

3. Canonical Definition

Engineering Confidence is the explicit expression of the degree of certainty associated with engineering cognition, based on the quality, completeness, consistency, and applicability of supporting information.

Engineering Confidence represents confidence in a conclusion.

It does not establish that the conclusion is objectively correct.

4. Characteristics

Engineering Confidence SHALL possess the following characteristics.

4.1 Explicit

Engineering Confidence SHALL be represented explicitly whenever practical.

4.2 Evidence-Based

Engineering Confidence SHALL be derived from available Engineering Evidence and supporting reasoning.

4.3 Context-Dependent

Engineering Confidence SHALL be interpreted within the applicable Engineering Context.

4.4 Dynamic

Engineering Confidence MAY increase or decrease as Engineering Evidence changes.

4.5 Traceable

Engineering Confidence SHALL remain traceable to the evidence and reasoning from which it is derived.

4.6 Explainable

Engineering Confidence SHALL be explainable through identifiable contributing factors.

4.7 Comparable

Engineering Confidence SHOULD support comparison between alternative engineering conclusions where appropriate.

